      "name": "Is my lubricant likely to be classified as an aspiration hazard?",
      "acceptedAnswer": { "@type": "Answer", "text": "Aspiration hazard (Asp. Haz. 1 or 2 under CLP, corresponding to GHS Category 1 and 2) applies to liquid substances and mixtures that can cause chemical pneumonia if aspirated into the lungs. Hydrocarbon-based lubricants with a kinematic viscosity below approximately 20.5 mm²/s at 40°C are generally considered Category 1 aspiration hazards — this includes many light mineral base oils, some Group III base oils, and most low-viscosity process oils. Higher-viscosity lubricants such as gear oils, grease base oils, and high-VI engine base stocks typically fall above the viscosity threshold and are exempt from Asp. Haz. classification. The exact classification must be determined based on the kinematic viscosity of the product at 40°C and its composition — a mixture containing more than 10% of individual hydrocarbon components with kinematic viscosity below 20.5 mm²/s at 40°C may require Asp. Haz. 1 classification regardless of the bulk viscosity." }
